[Music]  long before highways and even before the  railroads canals helped the American  economy grow that time has long passed  but there's a new glimpse into what life  was like on these waterways after a  massive restoration Washington DC's CNO  Canal is once again welcoming boatloads  of visitors for a lesson on this  important chapter of American history  as this vessel takes visitors on a  time-honored Expedition squeezing under  low bridges and through narrow walls  it's 30 000 pounds are guided entirely  by hand this boat is a flat bottom has  no keel so it's it just Maneuvers like a  living room couch Franklin Santa Croce  captains today's trip on the Chesapeake  and Ohio Canal in Washington D.C so is  it okay that we're bumping into things  yes it's a canal boat it's designed to  bump into things Santa Croce moves the  boat much the same way his predecessors  did two centuries ago when this passage  revolutionized trade  the canal locks boats between heavy  Gates before deckhands change the water  level  floating the boat from one lock to the  next and allowing barges to move through  elevations  as it passes the historic Georgetown  neighborhood the boat retraces a chapter  of American History defined by both the  promise of Westward Expansion and gritty  perseverance  this Waterway helped modernize life for  Generations in the Mid-Atlantic by  providing a path for essential Goods  like coal  this spring a tour recreating the first  mile returned after 11 years as part of  a massive restoration are you nervous  when you took this first ride out oh  absolutely terrifying it's a lot of boat  I mean it's 80 feet long and 13 feet  wide and that pass we're going to be  going through is probably about 16 feet  wide powered by electric motors rather  than the mules that traditionally guided  a boat it tops out at a Brisk two miles  per hour but history takes its time five  houses down  is where George Washington negotiated  the purchase  of the land for the city of the District  of Columbia George Washington  1791. this was a stable back during the  Heyday of the canal Rex Carnegie heads  education Outreach for the Georgetown  Heritage Foundation which helped fund  the boat's return Why was it so  important to bring a canal boat back to  the CNO Canal here it's a good way to  connect folks to the history whether  that's recent history here in Georgetown  or whether that's the 200 year history  of the canal this is crazy the tours  have been deemed an instant hit nowadays  everything's so mechanical everything's  automated so seeing people actually get  out and use that muscle power that is  the connection to our ancestors here the  lock system in and of itself is  fascinating that's one of the reasons  why we always gather a crowd I mean  people are always excited to see this  boat either go up or come down that lot  a vision from George Washington himself  long before highways the Founding Father  imagined waterways linking the east  coast to the great lakes in Ohio River  the CNO Canal covers nearly 185 miles of  that mile zero marks the start planted  in front of a complex with its own  storied stamp on American history the  Watergate the canal then weaves along  dramatic waterfalls and through Mountain  tunnels on its way to Cumberland  Maryland its destination nestled in the  Allegheny Mountains the path opened for  trade in 1850 running alongside the  Potomac River let's go where we jumped  on the water with Christiana Hansen from  the CNO Canal National Historical Park  what did this area look like 200 years  ago so the Sano Canal it was a highway  you would have had bumper-to-bumper  boats the same way you would have had  bumper to bumper cars today going into  DC it was a very busy Place Hanson took  us to park headquarters in Williamsport  Maryland the Canal's halfway point this  is one of the original buildings along  the CNO Canal it is yes it's about 200  years old Canal boats would come up in  the Basin here and unload coal we would  then load the coal into the trains and  they would head off to go and power the  area  when Canal construction started the  project was an ambitious one and one of  the country's first large-scale civil  engineering projects  despite the promise of its name the  Chesapeake in Ohio Canal never made it  to the Ohio River Valley instead ending  in Cumberland Maryland after running out  of funds operators still found plenty of  coal there and it became an early supply  chain Heyday of the canal is the 1870s  this is right after we've had a civil  war so our country is rebuilding in  really new and different ways the canal  is really busy trade is expanding  the peak of its Heyday lasted about a  decade during the 1870s but the canal  operated for nearly a hundred years  communities grew on and along the water  as laborers including families work the  canal through all hours and Seasons some  of my favorite pictures are kids with  ropes around them that you go why is  that kid leashed oh it's so they don't  fall in what a childhood right but you  also wouldn't lead mules so the the big  engines on the canal boat were mules and  kids helped lead the them while Canal  trade thrived the railroad remained a  fierce source of competition eventually  overtaking it and after several  catastrophic floods the canal closed in  1924. is this in some ways a failure I  think it's a grand experiment so the the  canal it's a struggle to keep it running  and it was even back in the day you're  working against nature and that's what  they were struggling with in the 1800s  as well trains were getting better and  better faster and faster today it's one  of the most visited national parks  surpassing even Yellowstone as people  continue to honor a tradition of  exploring and dreaming of more this  canal the railroad the planes that are  flying overhead all of these things  connect us to that tradition and the  thing that we can hope for is that  things will become easier and more  efficient which is also ironic  considering how much we want to preserve  these chapter of American History so we  can learn from that absolutely that is a  story of the people not just the dates  and not just the famous folks but real  Everyday People how does that connect to  where we are and where we're going  and it really was revolutionary at the  time because either you don't really  have many options at that point in  History either you put it on on a cart  or a horse and it you can't really go  very fast or very far or you put on a  river and navigating those wild rivers  can be difficult so this really helped  open up a new way of life for so many  people more people visit than  Yellowstone that's amazing including me  that was one of my favorite places to go  when I lived in Washington I always  wanted to ride one of those you beat me  to it girl and mile mile zero the  Watergate yes  that was very cool very cool story stamp

